.\" uname.1 -- maux's uname(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An
.\" original page (this project's own text, not copied from a
.\" historic uname(1)), so it carries no third-party notice --
.\" MIT like the code, same posture as env.1. Rendered at build
.\" time to build/man/UNAME.TXT and staged on the play image as
.\" /usr/man/UNAME: `more /usr/man/UNAME`.
.TH UNAME 1 "September 14, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
uname \- print system identification
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uname
[
.B \-snrvma
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Uname
writes system identification to standard output. With no
options, it behaves as though
.B \-s
were given. Each option below selects one field; if more than
one is selected, whether by several letters or by
.BR \-a ,
every selected field is written on one line, space-separated,
always in the fixed order
.IR sysname ", " nodename ", " release ", " version ", " machine
regardless of the order the options were given.
.TP
.B \-s
The name of this implementation:
.BR maux .
.TP
.B \-n
The name of this node. maux has no hostname or network concept;
this is a fixed string,
.BR apple2e .
.TP
.B \-r
The release level of this implementation:
.BR 1.0 ,
a placeholder -- no kernel version is tracked anywhere in the
tree yet.
.TP
.B \-v
The version level of this release: also
.BR 1.0 ,
the same placeholder as
.BR \-r ,
for the same reason.
.TP
.B \-m
The hardware type this system requires:
.BR 65c02 .
.TP
.B \-a
Behave as though
.B \-mnrsv
were all given.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/UNAME  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
env(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0; 2 on a usage error (an unrecognised option, or
any operand -- this utility takes none).
.SH BUGS
Release and version are both the same fixed placeholder string
because neither is tracked by this build; either becomes a real
value the day a kernel version equate exists.
